OK, then lets talk about the middle item.

The 200 series machines actually seem to have *less* expandability
than the Multia, at least in terms of memory.  The Multias are listed
as being expandable to 256M & the 200's are only expandable to 192M.

At least the Multia case doesn't give you the impression that its
expandable.  I wouldn't even try to add a drive to these, but the 200
series case fakes you into trying to actually use those drive bays:

The case of the 200 is the worst 'workstation' case I've ever seen.
It took me hours to put a second disk drive in the center internal
bay.  When all was done, there was only 1-2mm clearence between the
power plug for the lower drive and the top front edge of the leftmost
memory simms.

Speaking of memory, I don't think the high-density simms will fit in
all 3 banks.  When installing 128M (in 2 64M pairs) I couldn't put a
high-density simm in the rightmost slot because there is a socket on
the motherboard that prevents you from angling in the memory board.  I
had to move the original 2 low-density simms to the rightmost pair &
doing that required removing the ethernet card, sound card & drive
bay.

On top of all that, its harder to put the cover back on a 200 than it
is a 5000/25 (or 3000/300).  It takes a few minutes of cursing and
grunting to make all the tabs & rails line up while the flimsy thing
is flexing all over the place.

I really don't need a tower case, but a case that's an inch or so
bigger in each dimension would make all the difference in the world.
I'm sure Digital is saving a little money by using these cases, but
they're probably loosing it right back in field service time.  

I'd kill for the same case as the 3000/400 or 3000/600.  Digital did
everything right with these.  Big, sturdy, metal, easy to take apart
and re-assemble.  There's enough space to work & easy add & remove
components. 

