   DECUS '95/WASHINGTON, D.C. TO TRANSMIT SESSIONS OVER THE INTERNET
                  Live Worldwide Transmissions To Utilize
                  The Internet Multicast Backbone (MBone)



SHREWSBURY, MA, March 30, 1995 -- The U.S. Chapter of DECUS, the Digital 
Equipment Computer Users Society, today announced they will utilize the 
Internet Multicast Backbone (MBone) protocol and transmission process to 
multicast sessions from DECUS '95/Washington, D.C., the premier seminars, 
symposium, and trade show event focused on the Digital marketplace. 

The pilot program will showcase just a few of the many timely and beneficial 
offerings presented at DECUS '95/Washington, D.C. via a live, worldwide, 
audio/video feed.

Those with access to MBone technology will be able to view live 
presentations from speakers including Internet pioneers Tim Berners-Lee and 
Marc Andreessen.

Berners-Lee, who will present "The World Wide Web" keynote, was part of the 
team which invented the World Wide Web.  With the number of Web applications 
and rate of usage exploding, Berners-Lee is expected to explain how he sees 
his invention changing in the coming years.

Andreessen, developer of the Mosaic browser, and vice president of 
technology for Netscape Communications, literally brought the Web out of the 
laboratory and into the mainstream.  During his session, "In the Internet 
Fastlane", Andreessen will describe the next leap forward in browsers and 
how we will be using the Web tomorrow.

Other featured sessions expected to be transmitted over the Internet include 
the event's "Keynote Address" delivered by Enrico Pesatori, vice president 
and general manager of Digital Equipment Corporation's Computer Systems 
Division; and "Industry Trends and Futures" a focus on the technical trends 
in the networks management arena, from networking and security expert, 
executive vice president of Network-1, Dr. Bill Hancock.

Transmissions are expected to be multicast Monday, May 8 at 8:30 A.M.; and 
Tuesday, Wednesday, and Thursday, May 9 - 11, at 8:00 A.M. Eastern Time.

More information on the MBone multicast from DECUS  95/Washington, D.C. will 
be posted, as it becomes available, on the MBone page of the DECUS Web 
server accessible via: http://www.decus.org/decus/events/dc/mbone.html/

MBone transmissions can be viewed through most computers running the UNIX 
operating system, utilizing a network interface designed to receive 
multicasts.  A set of tool applications, available from various FTP sites on 
the Internet, are also required.  More information on the MBone can be 
acquired online via: 
http://www.eit.com/techinfo/mbone/mbone.html/
http://www.research.att.com/mbone-faq.html/
ftp://genome-ftp.stanford.edu/pub/mbone/mbone-connect/
ftp://venera.isi.edu/mbone/faq.txt/

Addressing the critical issues of importance to today's Information 
Technology professionals, DECUS '95/Washington, D.C., May 6-11, 1995 at the 
Washington Convention Center and Renaissance Hotel, will provide the 
real-world solutions necessary to optimize today's distributed computing 
enterprises.

More information on DECUS '95/Washington, D.C. is available by contacting 
DECUS Customer Service at 1-800-DECUS55.  Outside the U.S. and Canada call 
1-508-841-7800.  Information is also available via: World Wide Web: 
http//www.decus.org/, Anonymous FTP: ftp.decus.org, and E-mail: 
mailserv@decus.org (send message HELP).

The U.S. Chapter of DECUS, the Digital Equipment Computer Users Society, is 
an Association of Information Technology professionals interested in the 
products, services, and technologies of Digital Equipment Corporation and 
related vendors.  The Association's purpose is to promote the unimpeded 
exchange of information, with the goal of helping its members and their 
organizations to be more successful.  The Chapter provides each member with 
means to enhance their professional development, forums for technical 
training, mechanisms for obtaining up-to-date information, advocacy 
programs, and opportunities for informal disclosure and interaction with 
professional colleagues of like interests. 

