In article <1995Mar30.114122.1@vax8.cfsan.fda.gov>, thc@vax8.cfsan.fda.gov
writes:
:
:My name is Ted Chambers and I work with the FDA in Washington DC.  We have
:recently replaced our VAX 8550 running OpenVMS with a single processor DEC
:Server 2100 (AXP) running OpenVMS.  We are primarily an IBM shop, but we use
:the DEC system(s) for scientific processing.
:
:I need to tell upper management a number that can compare the 2100 performance
:to that of IBMs, of course upper management thinks in IBM terms so they want a
:number of MIPS (Millions of Instructions per Second) for the 2100.  I have
:browsed the net and obtained some performance info, but none that specifies the
:AXP's performance in MIPS.
:
:If anyone can shed some light on this, I'd appreciate it.  Please E-Mail me
:directly.

   *Millions*?  How quaint.  :-)

   IBM instructions are different from Alpha instructions and can do
   differing amounts of work per instruction.  Direct comparisons of
   instruction rates across dissimilar architectures and instruction
   sets -- without factoring this difference -- are completely and
   entirely meaningless.

   Having said that, one can easily calculate a number for a completely
   meaningless MIPS comparisons based on the Alpha peak instruction rates
   quite easily: multiply the MHz times the number of instruction decode
   units times the number of processors in the box.

   In other words, the four processor AlphaServer 2100 4/275 peaks at
   4.4 billion instructions per second.  (Four processors, each running
   at 275 MHz, and each processor is dual-issue.)  The uniprocessor
   is good for (you guessed it) over a billion instructions per second.

   The prefered (and more rational) method of comparing systems from
   different vendors and across dissimilar architecutures is via either
   a site-specific or an industry standard benchmark.  (Common industry
   benchmarks are the SPEC series of benchmarks.)  Check with IBM for
   the SPEC numbers (usually SPECmumble92) for the particular model(s)
   you are comparing.

