newsgroups: comp.sys.dec

I wrote:

I'm trying to use DEC 320MB SCSI HD (RZ55) which came from a dead DEC 
workstation. I would like to connect it to Mac. There is a problem
though. The disk does not spin up when Mac boots. 

So far we found out that there is no jumpers and the only way is to change
the settings is from software, either from Ultrix or on PC. We can't find
anybody at Northwestern who uses DEC. We downloaded a program for PC
(RZSPINUP), but it only works w/ Adaptec SCSI controller, which we don't
have.

Would anybody know of other way to reprogram EEPROM on the HD, so it spins
up when powered up? Your info is appreciated.

William Jackson <miltie@ix.netcom.com> wrote:
>
>There is a program for a Macintosh that is a SCSI manager (something 
>like Silverlining?) that does the trick.  A guy I used to work with at 
>DEC who had the only Macintosh in the building was always spinning up 
>disks for me, the Rz55 was one of them.
>
>And yes, RZspinup will only work with an Adaptec controller and the ASPI 
>manager must be loaded.
>
>Bill Jackson
>bill_jackson@taec.e-mail.com
>

OK, we've solved the problem. Our version of SilverLining did not let us
change the EEPROM, but other people report that the newer version can do it
(we have v5.5, 93') by changing byte 3 of page 37 to 0x00. It's actually the
first byte you could change, since the first byte contains the page length
and the second one something else. We actually used SGI disk utility (fx) to
change the named byte. I agree that using RZSPINUP is much safer if it's an
option for you.

This question seems to be recurrent (may be due to the abundance of dead
workstations), so it may deserve a place in FAQ.

Leo



