In article <3kv1dj$356@news.sas.ab.ca>, tac@sas.ab.ca (C.H. Ta) writes:
>I need to set up a small Pathworks network which has about 10 PCs.
>
>Can a VAX workstation 3100 with 24 Mb memory is a good enough platform 
>to serve 10 client PCs ? I am not sure about the memory size needed. 
>What about performance issue, is 3100 workstation good enough for this
>task?
>

This is very much dependent on your applications and expectations.  Years ago
we started with 4 12-Mhz 386 on a MV II, and DEC claimed that the MV II could
handle 12 "typical" PCs.  We had a bottleneck due to the speed of the PCs and
the gross "network locking" implementation of one application, but everything
else was alright.

Over the years we grew to 35 PCs, a mixture of additional 12-Mhz 386, some
faster 386 (@5 -Mhz I think), some 33 Mhz 486s.  The MV II was swamped in
memory use (16 MB) and nearly overloaded in CPU.  Buying more memory almost
certainly would have led to overloadinge the CPU.

Just then PathWorks server for AXP was annonced, we bought the cheapest,
smallest AXP DEC offered at the time, and it's the CPU has been nearly idle
supporting all 35 PCs, but we added more memory once.

IMHO, if you already have the 3100 (you don't say which model, which covers a
broad range in the 3100 line), try it with a few PCs, measure, and procede
based upon measurement.

