In article <3l97pi$4ms@news.belwue.de>, schmatz@mix.caed.iao.fhg.de wrote:

[classic swap space problem deleted]

How much physical memory do you have?  32 MB?  64 MB?  Either way, I'd be
willing to bet you are in eager swap mode, and not lazy swap mode.  Try
removing the /sbin/swapdefault file, rebooting, and then monitor your swap
space periodically.  If it stays low, you are fine.

Eager swap reserves swap space at process startup; lazy only uses the swap
space as needed.  Lazy has the nasty effect of indiscriminately canceling
jobs when it can't find enough swap space, but this rarely happens on most
systems (we've never seen it happen with 2x physical memory, but we don't
run large jobs often either).

I wonder why lazy swap can't simply cancel the offending process, but I'm
sure I'm missing something here.

-- 
Brian E. Saunders                  saunders@castor.che.wisc.edu



