In article <3kv1dj$356@news.sas.ab.ca>, tac@sas.ab.ca (C.H. Ta) writes:
> I need to set up a small Pathworks network which has about 10 PCs.
>
> Can a VAX workstation 3100 with 24 Mb memory is a good enough platform
> to serve 10 client PCs ? I am not sure about the memory size needed.
> What about performance issue, is 3100 workstation good enough for this
> task?
>
> An alternative is to buy new afphastation. Any suggestion on what
> workstation can do the job with the best price/performance?
>
> Appreciate for any ideas.
>
> Chung Ta
>
> Email: ta.chung@syncrude.com
>     or tac@dns.sas.ab.ca
>
>
--
Chung Ta,

I have seen many situations where 3100 class workstations (and smaller)
have been excellent Pathworks servers for modest numbers of PCs. I have
run many VS2000's as Pathworks hosts and have not had a problem.  The more
important consideration is workload.

Consider the cases of two environments with 10 PCs. In the first, the users
are constantly using their PCs, requesting access to data files and program
images. In the second, the PCs are turned on in the morning, and the users
spend the rest of the day sitting in their word processing or spreadsheet
programs. The workload of these two situations is drastically different.

As the workload gets heavier, the demand for resources also gets heavier.
In several cases, I have seen Ethernet performance be an impressive
bottleneck. The only way to be accurate (in your case), since you already
own the 3100, is to bring it up and see how the configuration performs. If
the CPU is very idle, and IO is low, there is no need to invest in a bigger
system at this point.

I hope that the above is helpful. If I have been unclear, or can be of
further assistance, please feel free to contact me via Email or phone.

