The VAXstation 3100 Model 38 does in fact have two SCSI busses, you can 
use the external bus to connect disks/tapes to the system.  You'll need 
the 68-pin Honda to 50-pin Champ connector adapter cable supplied by 
Digital (and probably available elsewhere) to connect up any standard 
external disk.  

The T50 command will go out and probe the bus for devices connected, and 
since it didn't find anything, will report back that it doesn't know 
what's out there.  Connect up the disk and see what happens, but make 
sure it's terminated properly.  


Finally, to build VMS on your satellite what you can do is this (if you 
only have one disk:  (things are less complex if you have an extra disk 
that will hold the savesets.  You could see if your page/swap disk would 
fit and boot the satellite diskless)

1:	Connect the disk to the cluster and restore the first VMS 
	saveset to the new disk.  When you're done, put the rest of the 
	savesets into the root directory on the disk where you've just 
	put the base VMS

2:	Boot the new disk that you just restored and when it asks you
	where the distribution is held answer with the name of the
	system disk

This procedure takes a fair amount of disk space since you have to 
restore the savesets to the same disk that contains the savesets.  
However if you have the space (and I've never done this with V6.x so I 
don't know what it takes) it works just fine.


Make sure you've got all of the licenses etc, I know your VAXstation 
came with a VMS/Cluster/DECnet license for V5.x, but don't remember what 
Digital did when they went to V6, so make sure you're OK before 
starting.  (LMF will ask for the license keys!)


