|>I Have an alpha 166 MHz AXP PCI/ISA motherboard computer with 32MB of
|>RAM and 2G harddirve Seagate hardrive.
|>I have installed windows nt 3.5. Everything was running fine until  I
|>tried  to connect the Machine to our Lan (netware 3.12)
|>I could not configure any ISA card or have NT configure it. The
|>Network cards I tried were  3com 3C509, intel etherexpress flash
|>SMC elite 16 and GVC NE2000 card. Please Help!!! My next move willbe
|>to try PCI network cards.

Did you verify that those ISA adapters are on the hardware compatibility list
with Alpha system support?  If not, then you should check with the vendor to
see whether they support Alpha's.  As for PCI network cards, there are a
number of them to choose from, but again, verify that they support Alpha.

|>One last question how does one find out what IRQ's,  the PCI slots are
|>configured for and also the NCR SCSI
|>controller. The manual that came with the board is very Skimpy I wish
|>Digital would have done a better Job.
|>Thanks  in advance !!!

You can run WINMSD.EXE to see what resources the various devices have
registered.  However, realize that ALL PCI devices use one of four interrupt
lines (INTA, INTB, INTC, and INTD).  The majority of single-function PCI
devices use INTA.  That means that Intel-based IRQ's don't really mean a
heck of a lot to a PCI device.  On your Alpha, the PCI devices will get
assigned an Alpha-specific interrupt number while the ISA devices will map
from the Intel-based IRQ to an Alpha-specific interrupt.
