In article <3kn5n2$gl1@jac.zko.dec.com>, hoffman@xdelta.enet.dec.com (Stephen Hoffman) writes:

:  I don't have an AlphaServer 1000 manual handy to check how to halt
:  the system, but there's usually either a halt button on the system
:  enclosure (often with a circle-triangle icon), or one can often press
:  the <BREAK> on the console terminal.  If you got an AlphaServer 1000
:  manual with the system, please check there.

  As was (correctly) pointed out to me in some offline e-mail, one should
  typically perform a controlled shutdown using "shutdown -h now" on a
  running UNIX system -- (if memory serves) the shutdown issues the synch
  calls necessary to synchronize the in-memory caches with the disks, and
  otherwise cleans things up.

  The console or front-panel <HALT> can be used after the shutdown, or
  after powerup and before UNIX has gotten going on the AlphaServer, and
  should generally be avoided while UNIX is up and running.  If you do
  <HALT> the system, you'll probably need to issue an fsck call or two
  from single-user mode to repair most (all?) of the problems that might
  be caused by a halt.

  OpenVMS can typically be halted on-the-fly, and it will reboot without
  problems; it will perform the OpenVMS equivilent of the fsck operation
  automatically.  However, a proper shutdown can avoid various potential
  application synchronization problems should applications have their own
  shutdown commands located in SYSHUTDWN.COM, and it can speed the reboot
  by cleanly dismounting the disk volumes.

