>We're using a MICROVAX 3100 -80, 90, 95, as well as
>VAX 4000-100 and 105A. All these machines suffer from 
>sporadic crashes and/or devices going into the dreaded "mount/verify".
>We have DEC and Seagate SCSI hard disks, as well as Exabyte and 
>DEC TF85/86/87 and at one time or another each of these devices
>is suspect. Various Digital technicians have said that the SCSI cable
>length is critical, and it seems this is a common problem on Microvaxes.

Dear Greg,

We have been using scsi for years on these types of platforms using LAVC
all disks shared and in a mixed cluster environment (axp and vax).

I'll assume you are running VMS, probaly LAVC cluster, and you are not 
using a 9.0GB disk (seagate on pre ver 6.0 of VMS.

My experience is that look at AUTOGEN, ethernet and exabyte 8200's.  Some 
sporadic crashes are caused by low system resources.  Mount verifys used
to be the norm at our site.  Low resources and timeout parameters are one
culprit.  Second is glitches on your ethernet.  If a system on your net
(including pc's) starts babbling for a short period of time, then one or
more of your vaxes will lose connection for a short period of time with a
disk.  If scsi/lavc timeouts are too short then you will get mount verify
timeouts.  Some of our timeouts are set to exceed 24 hours.

The other problem we have seen is with exabyte 8200 drives on DEC scsi
(not 3rd party q-bus scsi) buses i.e. VS3100, VS4000 etc.  On these systems,
crashes were sporadic, no particular reason, except, that they occured when
using NFS data transfers to our Sun's.  Without the 8200's on the VAX's there
were no problems.  TKZ09's and 8200's on emulex scsi have had no problems.

I hope this helps.

Gary Muswick
muswick@max.cwru.edu
 


