with 5.6) since Version 5.6 became available.

     Like many other RT-11 users, I'm sure, I was formerly an "SJ" junkie.  I
ran SJ because it was the smallest monitor, and thereby gave me the most core
for my executable.  I wasn't into writing code that made use of extended
memory; in RT, I tended to use that as VM:, while in TSX+, this was where my
multiple processes lived.

     SJ is now gone, of course.  I initially missed it, as I had programs
crash in SB for lack of memory.  These tended to be compiled executables
(typically Pascal programs), and I didn't look forward to "writing smaller".
However, the solution was trivial -- I simply switched to one of the Z
monitors and ran in "virtual" mode (VRUN), thereby giving my program MORE
memory (as the monitor vanishes from User mode, leaving you with 56K).

     Now I'm getting greedy.  I'm presently rewriting a data acquisition
program, which was straining at the seams, to take advantage of separated I/D
space.  Not sure what I'll do with 112K.  [And, if I need just a smidge more,
