	I wrote:

	>> Now, a good question is why DEC includes a 16" color
	>> multiscan monitor in the cost of a DEC 3000m300LX package.

	Dan Pop <danpop@cernapo.cern.ch> replied:

	> Because the 3000/300LX is sold as a _workstation_ and not as
	> a batch server.  And, while 32 MB might be enough for a
	> batch server, they certainly aren't for a workstation.

DEC could advertise an even lower price for a DEC 3000m300LX package
and still call it a "workstation" if they dropped the monitor (and let
people buy their own from whatever sources they want).  If I purchase
a PC from DEC, I get to select the monitor seperately -- why not do
the same with a DEC workstation?

One person's "workstation" is another's "batch server".  Notice that
DEC doesn't offer a DEC 3000m300LX in any "batch server" configuration
(at least not in the CERN offer, which is where I assume you are
getting your data).  Should I conclude that the DEC 3000m300LX is not
a batch server, then, because DEC doesn't package it as one?  If I
want a "batch server", to I have to buy DEC's higher priced offerings?

In terms of cpu-per-buck, the DEC 3000m300LX comes out ahead of most
(all?) other DEC workstation offerings, based on the prices I have
seen.  It is a cheap box, with good price/performance ratio.  I don't
much care if it is being marketed as the next best home space heater
-- if it works well as a batch server, the price/performance is good,
and the machine suits my needs, I'll buy one (or more!).


	> A system with with a 21" color monitor is certainly not sold
	> to be used as a batch server, yet it is offered with half
	> the memory needed by a workstation configuration.  This is
	> my gripe and I claim it is a sensible one.

I agree with the claim that 32meg isn't enough for running X-windows
on an Alpha workstation (at least on VMS, probably also on OSF/1
... err Digital Unix).  I do not agree that making 64meg the default
base configuration is necessarily good for (or wanted by) all
customers.  I am happy to have bought 32meg DEC 3000m300LX nodes -- it
is all my group needed, and we can purchase additional RAM at a much
lower cost from a third-party vendor (less than 75% of the cost of
DEC's RAM).  While I would have preferred not having to pay for the
monitor as well, they are nice enough multiscan monitors and I have
easily found uses for them.  It just seems silly that I would >have<
to buy a DEC monitor in the package.

I do think that DEC should point out to customers that 32meg is too
little for running X-windows.  Then the customer can buy the
workstation package plus a 32meg upgrade.  However, the customer can
>choose< who to buy the RAM from.

(I am also not yet convinced that a full 64meg RAM is needed for
interactive work on a DEC 3000m300LX running VMS and DECwindows/Motif.
I am currently testing a 48meg system this way -- and the results
aren't too bad so far.  32meg is too little for DECwindows/Motif in
our environment, though.)


	> The customer who hasn't used such a system before has no
	> clue that 32 MB are not enough for a single user
	> workstation, so s/he'll find out this the hard way and will
	> have to order more memory later.  The net result is an
	> annoyed customer [...]

While I agree with this sentiment, I think that nobody should trust a
computer company to say what the bare minimum configuration is for a
workstation or PC.  Customers' needs are too varied to make this
effective.  The ideal tactic that a customer should use is get advice
from people who have already used the systems -- from net-news, from
the web, from people at the same site, etc.  Then, based on multiple
recommendations from people who have used the equipment for real-world
tasks, a more informed decision can be made.

Given the complexity of buying computers today (and the arrival of
snake-oil companies in places like the PC industry), the customer
needs to be careful when putting down several thousands of dollars (or
Swiss Francs).  It is easy to get screwed by a car salesman -- and it
is becoming easier to be screwed by computer salesmen.  While packaged
systems are a nice concept, you end up getting only what is in the
package.  If your needs do not fit the package, you may have wasted a
lot of money.

You can also ignore the "package" and look at the real benefits, and
maybe find a good "batch server" in the wolf's "workstation" clothing.

						.oO Chris Oo.

p.s.  I have actually had DEC workers at CERN recommend that I buy
more RAM for certain systems, offering that the computer wouldn't work
well without it.  I declined the extra memory (based on some
assumptions on what kind of jobs we would be running), and haven't had
any problems.
