>: Are they analog or digital RGB-monitor, and what are the frequencies ?

>:  Can they by any means be used on a PC ?

These monitors are 1024x864,  54.054 kHZ horizontal, 60 Hz vertical, 70 MHz 
bandwidth. These are not multisync monitors. Internal frequency can be 
adjusted about +/- 3%.

Use on a PC ? Well, in most cases no, so far as I know. The PC card would have 
to be capable of producing composite sync on green. Most video cards produce 
separate vertical and horizontal sync only. Cards based on Texas TMS34010/020 
chips are in theory capable of producing just about any output, but not all 
cards have the necessary additional hardware support to do this. Perhaps 
readers can advise on other popular chips ...  eg ATI Mach32, Tseng and others.

The problems are that 
a) a PC expects to see VGA registers at startup (ie from the video 
BIOS) and to output 640x480 (but the monitor expects exactly 1024x864...).
b) any higher resolution (800x600, 1024x768, 1280x1024) requires a suitable 
device driver, *once*  DOS has started.
c) Windows requires a device driver (and all the baggage that goes with it).

So using a DEC monitor on a DOS/WINDOWS PC is out unless you have the 
programming skills in device drivers.

I an told that LINUX has some capability in programming the video device 
registers. I am not a LINUX user so I don't know the details.

