It depends on what you mean by "decent performance", I guess.  A 32meg RAM
DEC 3000 Alpha workstation works just fine as a batch machine if you
don't run DECwindows/Motif.  We have several 300LX models hooked up to
a single VT220 with a serial switch box which we use as a CPU farm --
all have 32meg RAM, and our applications run just fine.

VMS/Alpha runs fine in 32meg RAM for >some< types of usage (i.e. those
not requiring a lot of memory or DECwindows/Motif).  Requiring the
bare-minimum VMS/Alpha box to have 64meg RAM would just raise the
price, and some groups (like mine) don't necessarily need all of that
RAM.  We are quite content that the base-level machine starts with
32meg RAM.  [Besides, it is cheaper to buy the additional 32meg RAM
from a vendor other than DEC, if you need it.]

Now, a good question is why DEC includes a 16" color multiscan monitor
in the cost of a DEC 3000m300LX package.  I personally wish they would drop
the monitor and the price a bit.  It looks like that have done exactly
that with the new low-cost AlphaStation offering.

