In article <sawD5G7z1.1u9@netcom.com>, saw@netcom.com (Stephen Weinstein) writes:
|Has anyone run LAT over a  frame-relay network.  We are currently running
|combined LAT,IP and IPX traffic over point-to-point lines.  We plan to
|switch to frame-relay.  We did a test and found LAt response was poor
|for 56k AND ABOVE and some users experienced disconnects.  We have not
|been able to get a response from DEC as to whether we should run LAt over
|the frame-relay network.  If you have tried this and got it to work, or
|can provide a list of things to check and change, it would be appreciated.

Haven't done LAT over FR, but have done LAT over T1 in a large,
complex network.  In general, when doing LAT over a WAN:

1. Consider not doing it!  (Better to use TELNET where possible)
2. If doing it, ABSOLUTELY make sure you max out the following timers:
   RETRANSMIT LIMIT to the max (120 sec?)
   KEEPALIVE TIMER to the max  (255 sec?)
   This will give you the best chance of not seeing disconnects.
   Do this on both the server side and the client side.
3. Probably want to boost circuit timer (to maybe 300 ms) if doing
   bulk data over LAT over high latency WAN
4. Get smart boxes to bridge the LAT between WAN and LAN.  Ones
   that can prioritize LAT over protocols are a big win.  Ones that
   can compress LAT data are a plus, esp. if WAN bandwidth is scarce.
5. Consider a box that actually acts as an application-layer gateway
   between LAT and a WAN protocol.  Such a box can terminate the actual
   LAT sessions at the LAN border, and app-gateway the apparent session
   via, e.g., reverse TELNET for the WAN.

Hope this helps,

Aaron

