>It depends on what you mean by "decent performance", I guess.  A 32meg RAM
>DEC 3000 Alpha workstation works just fine as a batch machine if you
>don't run DECwindows/Motif.  We have several 300LX models hooked up to
>a single VT220 with a serial switch box which we use as a CPU farm --
>all have 32meg RAM, and our applications run just fine.
>
>VMS/Alpha runs fine in 32meg RAM for >some< types of usage (i.e. those
>not requiring a lot of memory or DECwindows/Motif).  Requiring the
>bare-minimum VMS/Alpha box to have 64meg RAM would just raise the
>price, and some groups (like mine) don't necessarily need all of that
>RAM.  We are quite content that the base-level machine starts with
>32meg RAM.  [Besides, it is cheaper to buy the additional 32meg RAM
>from a vendor other than DEC, if you need it.]
>
>Now, a good question is why DEC includes a 16" color multiscan monitor
>in the cost of a DEC 3000m300LX package.

Because the 3000/300LX is sold as a _workstation_ and not as a batch
server.  And, while 32 MB might be enough for a batch server, they
certainly aren't for a workstation.  Here are the DEC packages:


Part Nr.        Mem     Disk    Graph   Monitor CD      O/S
------------------------------------------------------------
PE32B-BA        32      535     HX      17"C    N       OSF
PE32B-CA        32      1050    HX      21"C    N       OSF
PE32B-NA        32      535     HX      17"C    N       VMS
PE32B-PA        32      1050    HX      21"C    N       VMS

A system with with a 21" color monitor is certainly not sold to be
used as a batch server, yet it is offered with half the memory needed
by a workstation configuration.  This is my gripe and I claim it is a
sensible one.

The customer who hasn't used such a system before has no clue that 32
MB are not enough for a single user workstation, so s/he'll find out
this the hard way and will have to order more memory later.  The net
result is an annoyed customer, who can't get decent performance from
the workstation for a certain period of time, until the additional
memory arrives.  And this might be months, depending on the local
administrative overhead.

