In article <D56Lq2.8Mx@demon.co.uk>, Andrew Gibbs <agi%bsesoft@Britain.EU.net> writes:
|>Excuse my ignorance but I'm trying to read some datafiles into a PC.
|>They claim to be floating point and were obtained originally on an 
|>instrument based on some sort of DEC computer (PDP/Vax??).  All my 
|>attempts to read this data result in garbage and I would like to know
|>if the floating point format used is non-IEEE, and if so what is it?
|>(bit allocations for mantissa/exponent/sign(s) etc)

I'll assume it's a VAX, though some PDP-11s had similar layouts.

Single-precision:

	Exponent: 8 bits, bias 128, bits 7:14 (little-endian)
	Mantissa: 24 bits, normalized, MSB hidden, 0<m<=0.5
	  MSB 7 bits are in bits 0-6, little-endian; next 16 bits are in 16-31.
	Sign: 1 bit (zero for positive, one for negative): bit 15
	Zero: sign and exponent zero, mantissa ignored

	seeeeeeeemmmmmmm   Bits 0:15
	mmmmmmmmmmmmmmmm   Bits 16:31

Double-precision (D_float):

	Just like single precision but with an extra 32 bits of mantissa
	in bits 32-47 and 48-63

These are the most common formats.

The tricky part is that the mantissa layout is strange - the most significant
bits are in the lowest-addressed bytes.  This layout "looked" normal on
the 16-bit PDP-11, but seems strange on a 32-bit system.  For conversion
to IEEE, don't forget the different position of the radix point and the
different exponent bias.

For single precision, you could probably get by with swapping the 16-bit
words and multiplying by 4. 
