Campuswide Software License Grant (CSLG) program. If you use DEC software
now or are considering using DEC software in the future, please read the
following information about the CSLG program.

  A public meeting was held to discuss the CSLG issue on March 2. About 15
DEC sites were represented at the meeting. This document contains both a
report on the meeting and some additional information about the CSLG issue.

  Ron Thiel, DEC Marketing Executive, made a short presentation. He said
that the CSLG program provides annual software licenses and annual rights
to new versions for all layered products included in the CSLG Product List.
The list includes a very large number of DEC software products which run on
the following DEC (and other) platforms: OpenVMS VAX, OpenVMS AXP, DEC
OSF/1 RISC DEC OSF/1 AXP, Ultrix/VAX, Ultrix/RISC, DOS, OS/2, Windows,
Sun, Macintosh, and Windows NT.

  Copies of the complete CSLG Product List were distributed at the meeting.
The list may also be obtained from the UTIRC ftp server at
"ftp.utirc.utoronto.ca" in directory "/pub/CSLG". The directory also
contains other (previously distributed) information about the CSLG program.

  Thiel also pointed out that existing CSLG licenses will expire on May 1.
After further discussion it was generally agreed that the University should
enter into the proposed CLSG agreement as soon as possible so that new
Product Authorization Keys (PAKs) can be distributed in the very near
future. The target date for signing up participating systems is March 31.

  At this time it appears that something like 26-50 systems will
participate in the program. If so, the total annual cost of the CSLG would
be the following:
    UofT membership (assuming 26-50 systems)        14000
    Portfolio charge (CD-ROM containing PAKs)         700
    Taxes and administration (about 20%)             3000
    TOTAL:                                          17700
In this scenario the annual cost per system would be $350 and $680,
depending on the actual number of participants.

  One of the topics discussed at the meeting was the possibility of
including DEC's Media and Documentation Distribution Service (MDDS) in the
agreement. At the time it appeared that obtaining binaries and online
documentation in this way would be financially attractive. However, during
the last week we have discovered that the price being discussed was for
only one product category, not the whole set. Moreover, the MDDS issue can
be reopened at a later date.

  CSLG sign-up forms were handed out at the meeting. A few of them have
been filled in and returned already. To obtain copies of the form or to
obtain additional information about the CSLG program, please call Amanda
Wagner in the UTCC Software Distribution office (978-4990).

  If you have technical questions, you may contact me (see below).
[However, I will be gone until March 26. During that time contact Norman
Wilson at "norman@utirc.utoronto.ca" or 978-6358.]

