In article <bschor.44.03CD5D35@vms.cis.pitt.edu>, 
    				bschor@vms.cis.pitt.edu (Bob Schor) writes:
[question about PDP/VAX floating point format deleted]
> 
>      The PDP-11 single-precision floating point format is similar to the PC 
> single precision format.  There are two differences -- an "endian" problem 
> and a "hidden precision" problem.

        I think it's confusing to say  this is an "endian" problem since
    both  Intel,  and  PDP & VAX, use "little-endian"  addressing.   You
    could say it's _similar_ to an endian problem, but it  applies  only
    to the floating point format and only at word (16-bit) granularity.

        The reason for this seemingly odd  format is that it allowed the
    word  that  contains the sign bit (MSB word) of the 4-byte float  to
    sit in R0 and set the hardware status register flags on the PDP (and
    VAX).  If the float was constructed in the "natural"  4-byte  order,
    the   sign  bit  would  appear  in  R1,  would  not  set  the  flags
    appropriately (for a float) and the  test for negative or zero would
    have  had to be done in software (slowing things down a lot!).  It's
    a bit of a hack, but it makes sense for the hardware on which it was
    developed (and the VAX hardware maintained  compatibility  with  the
    PDP format).

        Secondly, it's not  a  "hidden  precision"  problem (I think you
    meant   hidden  _bit_,  BTW),  but  rather  a  different  choice  of
    _normalization_.  Both IEEE and PDP/VAX floating point formats use a
    "hidden bit" in the mantissa, but IEEE chose to normalize as  1.bbbb
    whereas PDP and VAX use 0.1bbbb (and only the "bbbb" are represented
    in the mantissa bits).  In addition, IEEE chose to use a bias of 127
    (versus  128  on PDP/VAX) for the 8 bits of exponent which gave them
    (1) symmetric value range and  (2)  some extra exponent values which
    can  be used for NaN, Inf, etc.  So you get one factor of 2 from the
    binary normalization and a second factor  of  2  from  the  exponent
    bias,  resulting  in  a  factor-of-4  difference  between  IEEE  and
    (re-ordered) PDP/VAX float.  Single precision only, BTW.

>      Assume you have a four-byte PDP-11 single-precision floating point 
> number.  Do the following:
> 
> 1)  Store the number into a PC floating point variable, but in word-reversed 
> order.  That is, the first 16 bits (word) from the PDP go into the second 16 
> bits on the PC, and vice versa.  This solves the "endian" problem.
> 
> 2)  Divide the (PC) floating point number by 4.  This solves the hidden bit 
> problem.

        Nice prescription.

            -Ken
