levy@fndcd.fnal.gov wrote:
: > uh oh... ms-dos is fine for a pc-xt... but take a look at that over-aged
: > concept. 

: Over-aged?  Unix is just a tad older than MS-DOS.  And it shows it.

The above statement is very misleading. Unix has been almost
completely rewritten (kernel-wise) in recent years, and contains
very modern concepts, including shared libraries, loadable kernel
space module (eg. lkm, STREAMS modules), virtual memory with memory 
mapping (mmap()), local and remote inter process communications,
remote file sharing, real time processing support, dynamic data
structures, multi-national language support, virtual file system, 
and a true preemptive kernel. Add the concepts of multi-user,
multi-group, multi-tasking, and multi-threaded.

Unix (as a loose term, to include SVR4, *BSD, OSF, and others) 
also continually evolve, including a movement towards a smaller
kernel (microkernel), SMP multi-processing support, and 64-bit 
structures (as opposed to, say, the 32-bit WNT.) Even little
things like long-filenames (255 chars, compared to 8+3).

How much more modern do you want ? Tell me how that compares
with MS-DOS or Win NT or VMS ? To say that the concepts in Unix 
is comparable to MS-DOS is plain ignorant. This discussion 
includes the treatment of security in both operating systems.
(MS-DOS doesn't even have any notion of users or passwords,
much less per-user access restrictions.)

-Ade Barkah
