Douglas Taylor (dtaylor@summit.bor.ohio.gov) wrote:

: We are currently trying to establish SLIP connectivity for our server using
: 	the /usr/new/slattach program provided by DEC.  The current testing
: 	machine is a Macintosh, and the version of ULTRIX which is hosting
: 	the SLIP connection is ULTRIX 4.3.1

: The problem arises when trying to use any client program from the SLIP
: 	initiator.  One can only telnet to the host using the IP unresolved
: 	address; one cannot telnet elsewhere.  Too, one cannot telnet to
: 	the host using a resolved name - only the address in the form of
: 	XXX.XXX.XXX.XXX.

You need to add a proxy arp on Ultrix to correspond to the dispensed
SLIP IP address of the Mac.

: We have verified with almost total certainty that the initiating machine (in 
: 	this case, the Macintosh) is configured correctly.  The problem seems
: 	to be on the server end.

Yes.  There is a bit of housekeeping to do to get your routing to work.

: Possible suspicions are the netmask being set incorrectly: would this
: 	have the effect of blocking name-resolution?  

: Any  help would be appreciated.

	/etc/arp -s <Mac-Host-Name> <Ultrix-Ethernet-Address> pub

This will create a permanent (published) entry in the ARP tables
of the Ultrix host enabling it to act as a proxy for all IP packets
addressed to the SLIP client.  Without this bit of housekeeping,
the rest of the world (including the NameServer) has no idea where
to send the packets, as no one was answering the ARP (Address
Resolution Protocol) for the Mac's SLIP address.  Read the man pages
on arp for mor information.

Barry Kort




