>>>>> "Richard" == Richard F Lukes <rflukes@silver.cs.umanitoba.ca> writes:
In article <3jn41g$rin@canopus.cc.umanitoba.ca> rflukes@silver.cs.umanitoba.ca (Richard F. Lukes) writes:

Richard> I have a question about using both 32MB and 8MB memory
Richard> modules in a DECstation 5000/200 workstation.  For many years
Richard> we ran a DS5K/200 with 40MB using a Kingston 32MB memory
Richard> module in slot #0, and an 8MB memory module in slot #1, with
Richard> no problems.  Recently we had our motherboard replaced by DEC
Richard> and now we get a "cpu 0 panic: unknown memory error
Richard> interrupt." error message.  Our new BIOS version is 5.3T.  Of
Richard> course, DEC's answer is, you can't mix and match modules, but
Richard> according to Kingston this should not be a problem.

DEC has never officially supported mixing of memory board types in
their 5000/[12]xx machines.  It was just a happy coincidence that it
did work.  I'm assuming that you can run the machine with just the
32Mb module in there?  If so, have you tried using it with just the
8Mb module as a test?  From the sound of it, I would sorta guess that
the Kingston board isn't quite upto the DEC spec, and the old
motherboard was more tolerant.  But I can't really say unless you get
me some more information.  

Richard> Any ideas as to why this isn't working?  Also, I never
Richard> understood why you could not mix the various sized modules.
Richard> The machine has dynamic address translation, so surely it
Richard> would have been trivial to write a BIOS which could use any
Richard> combination of memory, so long as the 32MB modules came
Richard> before the 8MB modules in the RAM bus.

It's the way the memory bus was laid out at the time.  The system
looks at the first memory board in slot zero, depending on the board,
it selects the mapping scheme for the slots.  With an 8Mb board, each
slot takes 8Mb of address space.  With a leading 32Mb board, each slot
takes 32Mb.  You can then have _one_ trailing 8Mb board because it
starts at the begining of the 32Mb slot.  If you put a board in the
next slot, it leaves a "hole" of 24Mb in the memory maps, which the
DECstations can't deal with.  At least that's how it was explained to me.

John

