In article <D52K11.Fwo@cogsci.ed.ac.uk>, anaru@cogsci.ed.ac.uk (Andrew Gillies) writes:
> We have a DEC Alpha 3000 500S running OSF1v3.0 that has a TLZ06 tape
> drive.  We normally use this on a regular basis for backups.  However
> we have just had a request from the linguists side of our department
> as to whether it is possible to use the drive to read real DAT "audio 
> tapes".  Basically all they require is to obtain the digitally recorded
> audio information from the tape as an audio file for analysis on the
> computer by a package called waves.

  Many DAT drives have an audio output connector and recognize audio DAT
tapes. For example, many Connor/Archive/Maynard drives operate this way -
if an audio DAT is inserted both front panel LED's blink and the audio is
available somewhere on the drive. Some models have a connector, while
others only bring it to test pads on the circuit board. Of course, this
doesn't give you computer control, digital sampling, or fast forward/
rewind capability.

  The only drives I know of that can read audio DATs as data are the Ar-
chive drives with custom firmware sold to SGI. I'm told you can buy these
drives as spares from SGI, but I don't know if the programming info is
available. I'd also expect the price to be substantially higher than the
price for the regular version of the drive.

  I don't know why the capability to return the digital audio on the SCSI
bus is so uncommon - many CD-ROM drives do this.

