I've been working in our application to make decent displays under X
on the various Alphas.  I was over at the DEC demo center on a variety
of machines trying to get a decent appearance.  Our major problem, is
that while the ALPHA can render fast enough for us, it doesn't have a
facility under X (that we can find) to draw to the screen synchronized
with the vertical retrace interval so we get terrible "tearing" or
"jaggies" in the appearance of our images.

Now, on the HP we solved this by switching over and using the X Multibuffer
extension, this seems to kick in the hardware double buffer support.  Even
then we had to set a special environment variable to enable the buffer swap
on the VBLANK.

On the DEC's (and we tried various ones including the newer graphic boards
that claim double buffer support), the X server implementation appears to
just do the standard sample code Multibuffer extension, which means that
not only is it not synchronized with the retrace, it's slower than all
git out because it does a couple of extra software copies that aren't
really needed.

Does anybody know how to get smooth (double buffered) performance on
these machines?  Can it be done under DEC's X?  Is there some other
kludge I can use?

Thanks.

-Ron



