In article <3k2fse$p58@paperboy.ids.net>, cormack_pl@ids.net (Michael Umbricht) writes:
>I have a VAX 11/750 that is showing memory errors.  Could someone explain what
>ARRAY BANK, ARRAY and ERROR SYNDROME = 2F are.

The array number is which board it is.


>Short of pulling the boards one by one and rebooting, how do I know which of
>the boards has the error?  (I have an extra board that I can swap in)  Is bank
>#3 the third slot?

Bank 3 refers to the bank within the array module.

>                                       ERROR SYNDROME = 2F
>                                       CORRECTED ERROR, BIT # 15.

The syndrome is the result of the ECC process, which indicates which bit needs
to be flipped to get the right data.  

>                                       ARRAY BANK #3. IN ERROR
>                                       ARRAY #3. IN ERROR

This indicates the board in the 4th slot (0, 1, 2, 3).  To verify, you could
shutdown, exchange the board in slot 3 with slot 2, and the board in slot 4
with slot 5.  That way, if I've guessed wrong (and it's likely; it's been years
since I've even *seen* a 750, let alone fixed one!), if the memory errors
persist, you'll have an indication of which slot it went to.

>                                      CORRECTED ERROR FLAG

Then again, the errors you're getting are corrected memory errors.  It may mean
that a cell in a chip, or a chip went bad, or that the bit within that cell has 
changed state.  That is, maybe that memory address or range of addresses is
read-only (once loaded at boot time, is never re-written), and so every time it
gets read, it gets another error.  Try rebooting and see if the problem
returns.  While you're rebooting, swap the memory modules around like I
mentioned earlier.  This will perform 2 tests: 1) the error is persistent, and
2) where it went.

