(Paul Nord) writes...

>We've got a 3000-600 Alpha system with Open VMS.
> 
>Generally only a couple of users on the system at a time.  Over the weekend,
>the boss called to say that he was having trouble compiling.  There was lots
>of disk access and it took a long time. 

Yes, your MONITOR screens show a memory problem.  Note, especially, the
high number of disk read i/o's per second.  

Four possible suggestions to solve the problem:

  1) Compile smaller modules. 

  2) Compile with /SEPARATE (if this is a FORTRAN application). A bunch
     of performance improvements were made and tied to this switch a few 
     months ago, and either have just recently shipped or will ship very
     soon.

  3) Tune VMS.  You don't say what compiler you are using, but there are 
     several that use the same compiler technology back end (I think the 
     list on VMS is Ada, BASIC, C, COBOL, FORTRAN), so the advice in the 
     DEC COBOL V2.1 Release Notes chapter on tuning Open VMS for large 
     compiles is for the most part generic advice.  Have a look there.

     (I wrote that chapter, with lots of advice from other folks, and in
     the process saw one compile speed up by a factor of 5x.  *But* after
     that initial win nothing I did seemed to help.  So try tuning, but 
     remember to stop trying after a decent interval.)

  4) Add memory.  The amount to add is not predictable without knowing 
     a lot more about your workload (sorry). 

Hope this helps

    /John Henning
