In article <3k2v1t$kqb@pt9201.ped.pto.ford.com>,
   fuller@pt9113.ped.pto.ford.com writes:

> 4/33 (figure out the processor type/speed...).  In that time, I can count the
> number of problems due to memory parity errors (remember, these systems have
> parity memory) on 2 or 3 fingers.
> 
> Now, given the fact that I had memory problems 2 or 3 times in the past 11
> years, would I consider the added cost and complexity of parity or ECC memory
> to be worth it?  Probably not.
> 

   By having parity memory you KNOW you had 2 or 3 memory problems,
without parity or ECC memory you could have errors and never know it, until
your database was corrupted, or your program produced the wrong result.

   I liken the danger of non-parity memory to the recent flap over the
Pentium FP problem. You can produce all the statistics you want that say
the average user will have a problem every N years, but the fact remains
that these situations increase the chance of an UNDETECTED error and those
are the worst kind of all.

   I'm not suggesting people must buy parity/ECC memory, but if you choose
not to accept that you have increased the change ( however marginally )
of a catastrophic error that you may not detect until it's too late to correct.
If the system is being used in a "mission critical" application I'd still
strongly recommend ECC ( or at least parity ) memory.

