In article <ewhite.1.00179184@gabriel.resudox.net>, ewhite@gabriel.resudox.net (elizabeth white) writes:
> Is anyone aware of a secure access system for DEC pc's using tokens
> (such as  SmartCards, TouchMemory or other technology)?  

MS-DOS is MS-DOS, right ?  I think this is independent of being a DEC PC.

SmartDisk Security Corporation (SDSCinfo@netcom.com) offers a product
called SafeBoot which encrypts the hard disk using a token (SmartDisk)
built in the form of a diskette (so you have a reader already).

I have not used this product, but I have studied the literature in
great detail for a prelude to using their high-end token (Crypto
SmartDisk) for authentication between systems.  I believe SafeBoot
has a worthy design for the appropriate problem space, but I think
if I were to go to the trouble to deploy it I would use it in the
more complex full-encryption mode rather than the simpler fast-install
mode which is most prominent in the manual. (Go ahead, you won't be
the first to accuse me of being a Security Fanatic. :-)

The vendor also offers a SafeBoot for Macintosh based on the same
principles.


