Previous replies removed:

In most cases, esp. with the MV/VS3100 line, the MV had four serial lines for
peripherals.  This changed with the 3100/76, which employed a different chip
set and did not have a MV counterpart.  There are similarities between the
MV3100/8x&9x and the VS4000-60/90  IMO, the VS4000 was nicer, as it had a much
more servicable chassis than the older style cabinets used in the MV.  Without
doubt, there are other minute differences.  As mentioned before, there were
different ID chips for the units.  The VS3100 had only two available serial
ports, as the other two were used for the mouse, et al.

Additional terminal ports on the MV3100 plugged into the same connector as did
the color and SPX graphics on the VS3100.  SCSI adapters plugged into the same
port on both variants.  Some of the options available were different, naturally.
Although memory for the VS3100 and MV3100 part numbers were different, I THINK
they would interchange, but don't make that official.  Additinoally, I believe
that the MV3100/80&90 series used the same MS44-XX memory as did the VS4000/60
and 90.  I can't recall if both the MV3100/80&90 offered the Turbochannel port
as in the VS4000/60&90.

In the MV/VS2000, I had been told that they were only different if the monitor
was present.  I'm not sure if they had dofferent ID chips.  In the case of ANY
VS based on the Q-Bus, the only difference would have been the presence of the
VCB0x graphics subsystem.  The VS3520/3540 was an entirely different animal, 
as it had a SMP CPU with two 3500 processors, a unique system bus that also
controlled the IO processor, the CPU, memory and graphics.  A Q-Bus adapter
was also available to connect a 5 slot Q-Bus backplane and an adapter that ONLY
attached a TK70 controller to the system bus was also offered

The VAX 4100/4105 was also a distinctly different motherboard, but used a very
similar chassis.  The "back" of the CPU box had many more IO options, as the
4100 offered external connections for both DSSI and SCSI.  The 410x could be
configured as a VAXStation by installing a Q-Bus adapter on the CPU and
installing the VCB02 in a seperate Q-Bus expansion cabinet.  The VAX410x also
used the MS44-xx memory (and may have required ONLY MS44-DA).

The VAXStation 8000 was based on a specially racked version of the 82x0/83x0
built with the assistance of Evans&Sutherland (spelling possibly wrong).  This
system was BI based, and contained many special interfaces to control the
additional hardware built-in by E&S.  This was a very short lived project, and
in my time with DEC I never saw one.  It had a unique control pad with two rows
of control knobs.  Not much more info that I have on this unit, as it was very
unique.

I've excluded the VS4000-VLC, as it didn't seem to share anything with any
other MicroVAX product.  It had it's own memory, video adapters, chassis and
power supplies  It offered performance similar to the VS3100/76, and was 
intended to be a low-cost unit.  Some have said that it missed that criteria
badly, but I'm not really sure.  It too only had two serial lines available
externally.  The VLC was limited to only 24MB physical memory  I'm not sure if
any of the other DEC emory vendors offered more, but there was a problem with
some that were too tall for the cabinet and caused shorting inside the chassis.

I'm sure that there are many more differences.  I'm also sure that there was no
intent to allow a conversion between the MV and VS systems.  I don't have detail
on anything about differences in chips used in either model.

Bob

STD Disclaimer:

My comments represent neither NASA or CSC...


