In article <3j2q8v$5hc@arc.electriciti.com>, jjo@triton.com (Jay Olson) writes:

> I am converting some VAX MACRO code to FORTRAN as part of an AXP conversion
> effort. Some of the entry points in this code are supposed to accept variable length
> argument lists. Is there any way in DEC FORTRAN for OpenVMS/AXP (or any other
> language) to determine the number of arguments a subroutine has been passed?
> 
> Note that this is the case where the number of commas may vary (e.g CALL XXX(A1,A2)
> and CALL XXX(A1) are both supposed to be allowed). I can use %LOC to determine if an
> argument has been left out, as in CALL XXX(A1,).

There's a couple of intrinsic functions that will be a lot of help.  In
6.1 of DEC Fortran was introduced:
 
        II = IARGCOUNT ()
 
which retuns just what it says.  Introduced with 6.2 of DEC Fortran was:
 
        PTR = IARGPTR  ()
 
which returns an INTEGER*8 address of the argument block as defined
by the calling standard.  More details about each in the Release Notes for
Versions 6.1 and 6.2 of DEC Fortran.


