I am an engineer in a radiology department looking at multiple solutions
to solve imaging problems and I have hit a number of roadblocks related
to this issue.

Most medical imaging applications do not need high speed graphics. 
(Yes, the realtime visualizations of beating hearts and 3D shaded maps of 
brain function are neat to look at for the lay person, and are extremely 
useful for research presenetations, but are generally not used by physicians 
clinically.  They are not standardized, sometimes difficult to relate to 
anatomy and in some cases cause confusion.  3D surgical planning does 
need high end 3D graphics, but I don't mind spending $10k to $30k on a high 
end board for one or two boxes.  Also, products like IDL offer 3D processing
in the CPU.)

What is needed in radiology is high speed painting of raster maps, and fast
palette control.  This can be in both grayscale or full color.  (We use
full color displays for moving overlays of different images, each in a
different color.)

DEC offered the TX board, which was low cost (relative to PXG+) at 24 bits,
but was not supported by VMS (so much for open OS,) only OSF.  We have run
VMS as the primary OS for several reasons.  Excellent cluster support,
realtime applications, an OS that does not crash due to programmer errors,
long term stability (typical system up time is 100 to 200 days, with most
system crashes due to a kicked out power cord,) shadowing, and one location
system management.  PC's on the other hand our worst nightmare.  Every PC is
different (maybe plug and play will fix) and tend to all need unique treatment
(not to mention that users modifying config.sys, autoexec.bat.) 
Unix systems are better, but still each has it's quirks.  There is a lot to
be said for single platform when programming and system management resources
are limited.

As I call around to display board manufactuers,  most do not even have NT
drivers, let alone Alpha NT or VMS drivers.   Yes, the ATI 2M DRAM card and
#9 cards, run on VMS, but at most 1280x1024x8bits.  What I need is low cost
1600x1200x8 or 24 bits for deployment of alot of viewing stations, in nursing
stations, emergency rooms, clinics, etc.  We are being forced by price, to
go to  PC's running windows to do this cost effectively.  I'd perfer to have
a bunch of Alphstations running VMS so I could control the environment better,
but solutions are limited.

So I need DEC to either make or provide drivers for non 3D video cards, that
can support 1600x1200 resolutions.  Not the limited 1280x1024 offered by the
more expensive 3D ZLX cards.  (BTW, I think that the ZLX is a great value
for what they offer, especially when compared to the compitition, but I don't
need the Open-Gl nor cost to license it.)

Gary Muswick
