As I have posted previously to other messages, VMS is by far a much easier
to maintain a lot systems with one person.  We have Suns, Mips, Vaxes, Alphas,
PC's 286 through P5 and MACs.   The one set of systems that do most work with
least resources (system management, programming) is the VMS cluster of Alphas
and Vaxes.  They also stay up the most, usually 100 to 200 days, most crashes
due to power cords getting kicked out.  The Mips are next, at +100 days,
followed by the Suns at a couples days to weeks.  And except for a PC not
doing anything, I have never seen one up more than a few days.  (Microsoft
was wise not to put a SHOW SYSTEM command into windows/dos to show uptime.)

I have found it easier to port Sun (non window) apps to VMS, esp. those from 
older Sun 4 systems than to from Sun 4 to Sparc, esp to Solaris.

I think what would help VMS (and DEC) is porting VMS to the Pentium or P6
processors.  There is what, some 30 million or more P5 processors.  You only
need .5 percent market share to get over 1.5 million new VMS users.  I can't
relate the system management gains from large bussinesses of a clustering
vaxes, alphas and pentiums togther.  Large organizations would gain far more
capability (and easier management) with VMS clusters, than with Pathworks.
Windows software could still run natively using a SoftPC product (but w/o
emulation, simialr to win apps on Pentium or 486 NT systems.)

I realize that DEC's goal on PC's platforms is NT which runs on Alpha, but
there is a long time before equivalent system management, security, display
functionality is found in NT.  (Clusters are 6 months or so away, the GUI is
going change either at or before version 4.0 etc.)

I know that the organization of the Intel processor, makes a VMS port harder,
but I think most low level stuff could be emulated.  Not ALL VMS functionality
is the same on Alpha and VAX.  There are slight differences that are documented.
The same could be true of an Intel version.

I know it would make my life easier.

