Here is something to look at based on our own bad experience.  We have an
isolation / power transformer supplying power to our computer room.  It
takes 416 Volt 3 phase (common in large commercial environments) and supplies
240/120 Volt single phase.  When the transformer was changed, the electricians
failed to ground the output of the middle leg of the system.  We had the
following hook up.


-    ---------------------------------------------------
 >||<                          ^                  ^
 >||<                         120V Outlets        |
 >||<                          v                  |
 >||<----  no connection   -------------         240V Outlets
 >||<                          ^                  |
 >||<                         120V Outlets        |
 >||<                          v                  v
-    ---------------------------------------------------

Because we had a lot of 220 volt systems, the power was usually balanced,
but after awhile with downsizing of computers (like getting rid of 11/750's)
the legs started to get unbalanced.  Power monitors at the Circuit breakers
and at the 240 outlets showed no problems.  But as the system became
unbalanced, one of the 120 Volt legs would got to 155 volts the other to
85 Volts.  The all sorts of weird things would happen.  Some computers
shutdown, others would blow fuses, others would keep working.   (Some new
power supplies automatically configure to work on either 120 or 240 Volt, I
didn't believe the documentation until I tried pluging it into both a 120 and
240 volt outlet (different cords).)  Since the power monitors showed no
fluctuation at the circuit panel, I had a hard time convincing our electricians
that our equipment wasn't at fault.  We lost 2 expensive supplies on non-DEC
equipment.  Most of our systems are Vax 3200 workstations with expanded 16 slot
q-buses.  Those systems must have very good supplies, since I watch voltages
fluctuate randomly between 85 and 155 and the system stayed up.

The other thing that also happend was that even after connection to of the
middle leg, they did not tie it to earth ground, leaving it isolated.  The
room is also supplied by standard 120 Volt outlets, not from the transformer.
Those outlets, including the raised floor was at earth ground.  I found this
little fact out after I touched the chassis of a tape drive (I was trying to
unjam a tape without the cover) and the edge of the 19 inch rack (which was
grounded to the raised floor.)  There was about 80 volts between the two.
We saw spikes to well over several hundred volts between the transformer
ground and the earth ground.  These only caused trouble on few pieces
equipment in the rack.  Guess what? Our electrician's power monitors never
looked for ground potentials above zero.  Again is was exasbertating to
get fixed.

So my guess is that, even though the power monitors, may have shown nothing,
you may still have a power problem.  I have not seen anyones equipment
especially DECs die like yours have.  Since IO cards can get connected to
external supplies, and audio to external systems, and video's to monitors
that could be getting power from a differnt source, or different ground
(even in the same room)  the liklihood for disaster is high.  The worst
part is, that what I have described is more likely to happen in a computer
room or lab where special steps were taken to provide clean power.

Gary James Muswick
Image Processing Engineer
