My theory:

The Alpha uses 8k direct-mapped first level caches to do its
work. I don't know what the HP uses.

In the case of the structure-based code, everything you want
for a given element is pulled in at the same time. In the
case of the array-based code, bad things can happen. For
example, assume that all of your arrays start on page boundaries.
In this case, if you want to access the same element location
from all the arrays, they'll map to the same line of the first
level cache, potentially causing lots and lots of cache
conflicts.

In this case, the larger (and more highly associative) 
second-level cache gets used to supply the data. So, it's
probably not an issue of memory bandwidth, but secondary-to-primary
cache bandwidth. It might also involve the main memory bandwidth,
but without a lot more information, I can't be sure (not that
I could, even with a lot more information).

|> What do you all think?  Are we right?  Do you have a better theory?
|> It's important that we know, because we may switch the array-based
|> code over to structures if we can get a 30-60% speedup out of it.

If your code uses the same element location from many arrays at
the same time (or close to each other), you're probably better off
switching to structures. The exact speedup you'll see depends on
a number of factors including #cache conflicts, memory bandwidth,
reuse, etc.

