In article <jbaker-0103952337050001@blv-pm2-ip19.halcyon.com>, jbaker@halcyon.com (James Baker) writes:
> I have a DEC TK-50 tape drive. Does anyone know what the inteface is? It
> has a 26 pin header. And is there a controller and/or software to use this
> on a PC?
> 
> I saw a review in Byte of some Quantum drives costing $4000+ that look
> similar so I started thinking I had something useful here. Then I came to
> my senses but I can still hope. 

  The interface is peculiar to the TK50/TK70 and their controllers. It's a
dual serial interface (one low speed for commands/status, one high speed for
data). As far as I know there are no controller implementations other than
the DEC ones (TUK50/TQK50/TQK70/BI-bus-combo-thingy).

  As the TK50 is only 95MB and the TK70 is only 295MB, there are other tape
devices that would be more economical for you. Either a QIC drive at the
low end (capacity up to 250MB at low cost) or a SCSI DAT drive would be a
good choice. Archive 4324's (4-8GB on a 90M tape) sell for about $700 new.

  The Quantum drives you've seen are higher capacity drives in the TF8 or
TZ* series (TF is DSSI, a proprietary DEC bus, while TZ is SCSI).

