In article <3j301q$lue@larry.rice.edu>,
Vivek Sadananda Pai <vivek@noel.cs.rice.edu> wrote:
>In article <3j0ljg$in7@agate.berkeley.edu>, peterm@tonks.EECS.Berkeley.EDU (Peter Mardahl) writes:
>
>|> What do you all think?  Are we right?  Do you have a better theory?
>|> It's important that we know, because we may switch the array-based
>|> code over to structures if we can get a 30-60% speedup out of it.
>
>If your code uses the same element location from many arrays at
>the same time (or close to each other), you're probably better off
>switching to structures. The exact speedup you'll see depends on
>a number of factors including #cache conflicts, memory bandwidth,
>reuse, etc.

Um, yes, but there is another solution: make each array slightly
bigger than it needs to be, so that each starts on a different
cache slot.  This is done automatically (!) by some run-time systems,
where they keep chaining information on allocated blocks, and means
that it is the very devil to compare cache-dependent programs on
different systems.

I don't know any system that uses hashing to access cache slots,
but it was certainly proposed some decades ago.  Nowadays it would
be quite cheap to do, and it would really confuse such benchmarking!


