In article <3j2q8v$5hc@arc.electriciti.com>, jjo@triton.com (Jay Olson) writes:
|>I am converting some VAX MACRO code to FORTRAN as part of an AXP conversion
|>effort. Some of the entry points in this code are supposed to accept variable length
|>argument lists. Is there any way in DEC FORTRAN for OpenVMS/AXP (or any other
|>language) to determine the number of arguments a subroutine has been passed?

DEC Fortran has the IARGCOUNT intrinsic for this purpose.  Please be aware
that the Fortran language does not provide for omitting arguments when calling
Fortran routines and that this is not really supported.  It works most of
the time, as long as the argument you omit is not CHARACTER or used in an
adjustable array bounds expression.  Be sure to declare VOLATILE any argument
which may be omitted.  For further details, see the DEC Fortran release notes.
