In article <Lam#m#_Tony_TY-2802951024040001@17.127.11.193>,
Lam#m#_Tony_TY@msgate.apple.com (Tony Lam) writes...

>I am supporting a manufacturing system on AXP platform running openVMS.
>As the workload increase, we are considering whether to add a CPU or
>go for a faster processing board. As far as I know, some application
>do not support dual-CPU. So how do I know about that if I got the source
>code of the application ?
> 
>Thanks
>Tony

You may need to further refine the word "support".  Some vocabulary that
I've sometimes heard includes:

  - tolerate: the application does not break in an SMP environment
  - decomposed: the application is structured to take advantage of
                parallel processing
  - licensed: some application license agreements may restrict you to
              using the application on one and only one CPU

If the application you are running uses one process per user and does
not share data structures between processes, I think you will find that
there are no particular SMP issues to worry about.  It's when you have
interdependent processes communicating with each other that you are
likely to discover that debugging them for a single-CPU environment is
rather different from debugging them for SMP.

As to which option will give you more benefit - a faster CPU will tend
to be better if you are doing one large job; multiple CPUs will tend to
be better if you are doing several (or many) things simultaneously.  But
check the current pricing before deciding; if there is a trade-in
program for whichever CPU you currently have to the faster CPU, that
might tip the balance.

