norris8016@delphi.com wrote:
: There is a ROM somewhere on the CPU that identifies it as either a MV or a WS.

Not true on all models.  On some models such as KA630 (Microvax II) the
console firmware senses presence of the graphics card.

To answer the original question --
A MicroVAX is a computer with VAX instruction set architecture, and no 
graphics console.  The console can be a printing terminal LA36 - LA75 or
a character video terminal such as a VT220.

A VAXStation is a computer with VAX instruction set architecture, AND a
graphics screen, keyboard and mouse directly connected to the CPU.
The important difference is that the License Management Facility usually
limits VAXStations to two users unless the system is in a University's
Campus-Wide License Grant.  Ie., You can't buy the 3-8 license upgrade
for a VAXStation, but you can for a microVAX.

A DECStation is a RISC architecture system, based on the MIPS R3000, R4000
or other MIPS CPU.




